{
  "review": {
    "score": 0.72,
    "summary": "SWE-agent presents a tailored agent-computer interface for autonomous software engineering and reports a substantial SWE-bench result, alongside comparisons intended to show that interface design matters. The contribution is promising and supported by reported benchmark and ablation findings, though the inspected material does not include enough evaluation detail to independently assess the strength or generality of those findings.",
    "observations": [
      {
        "kind": "strength",
        "claim": "The paper makes interface design, rather than model-weight changes alone, a central and concrete contribution, with tools for search, navigation, editing, and informative feedback.",
        "page": 2,
        "quote": "we introduce the concept of the agent-computer interface (ACI) and show how ACI design can lead to substantial improvements in the performance of LM agents, without modifying the weights of the underlying language model."
      },
      {
        "kind": "strength",
        "claim": "The paper reports a strong benchmark result and an interface comparison, including transfer to another language model. These are reported claims; detailed protocols and results were not available in the inspected pages.",
        "page": 2,
        "quote": "SWE-agent solves 10.7 percentage points more issues than the baseline agent that uses just the default Linux shell."
      },
      {
        "kind": "uncertainty",
        "claim": "The authors say the interface ablation uses a subset of 300 test issues. The inspected material does not show the selection procedure, full experimental settings, or the detailed table, so the robustness and scope of this comparison remain unclear.",
        "page": 2,
        "quote": "We perform an ablation study on a subset of 300 SWE-bench test issues to analyze our ACI design choices (Table 2)"
      }
    ],
    "unresolved_questions": [
      "How were the benchmark issues, model settings, compute budgets, and success criteria controlled across the headline comparison and the ablations?",
      "How stable are the ACI gains across different issue subsets, models, and software-engineering tasks beyond the reported SWE-bench setting?",
      "What limitations or failure modes do the authors discuss? The relevant discussion and most evaluation pages were not inspected."
    ]
  },
  "ledger": [
    {
      "check_id": "C1",
      "status": "mixed",
      "finding": "The inspected pages report a 12.5% solve rate on 2,294 SWE-bench issues, compare against a prior 3.8% result, and describe an ablation against a default-shell agent. The methods and detailed evaluation pages were not inspected, so setup comparability and supporting detail remain unknown.",
      "support": ["E1", "E2"],
      "counterevidence": [],
      "unknown": "Evaluation protocols, model and tool settings, baseline comparability, and detailed results."
    },
    {
      "check_id": "C2",
      "status": "mixed",
      "finding": "The paper reports a 10.7 percentage-point advantage over the default-shell baseline on a 300-issue subset and describes manual inspection plus a grid search on a development set. Detailed ablation results and controls were not available in the inspected text.",
      "support": ["E2", "E3"],
      "counterevidence": [],
      "unknown": "Full ablation table, issue selection details, experimental controls, and evidence across settings."
    },
    {
      "check_id": "C3",
      "status": "mixed",
      "finding": "The inspected pages establish the stated contribution and some reported results, but do not include the paper's full discussion or limitations. Eighteen of 25 pages are uninspected, and page 6 and pages 9–10 are only partial; no conclusion about unreported limitations is warranted.",
      "support": ["E1", "E2", "E3", "E4"],
      "counterevidence": [],
      "unknown": "Full evaluation, discussion, limitations, and later-page evidence."
    }
  ],
  "changes": ""
}

{
  "review": {
    "score": 0.76,
    "summary": "SWE-agent makes a concrete contribution by designing an agent-computer interface for repository-level coding tasks and evaluating its components. The paper reports strong SWE-bench results and useful Lite-set ablations, including comparisons with a shell-only agent. Evidence supports meaningful gains in the tested setup, while the headline comparison with earlier RAG results is not a like-for-like comparison, and the results’ generality remains uncertain.",
    "observations": [
      {
        "kind": "strength",
        "claim": "The paper clearly frames interface design as a contribution distinct from changing the language model, and implements that idea through repository navigation, editing, feedback, and context management.",
        "page": 2,
        "quote": "we introduce the concept of the agent-computer interface (ACI) and show how ACI design can lead to substantial improvements in the performance of LM agents, without modifying the weights of the underlying language model."
      },
      {
        "kind": "strength",
        "claim": "The evaluation reports 12.47% resolved on the full test set and component ablations on Lite. The same-model shell-only comparison on Lite (11.00% versus 18.00%) supports an interface benefit in that setting; the earlier RAG comparison uses different model results and is not a like-for-like comparison.",
        "page": 6,
        "quote": "w/ GPT-4 Turbo 12.47 1.59 18.00 1.67"
      },
      {
        "kind": "limitation",
        "claim": "The authors selected the final interface using qualitative analysis on hand-picked easier development examples, then swept some hyperparameters. This is a stated design procedure that warrants caution about how broadly the resulting gains generalize, not evidence that the reported test results are invalid.",
        "page": 5,
        "quote": "qualitative analysis of system behavior on small set of hand-picked easier examples from the development split of SWE-bench."
      },
      {
        "kind": "uncertainty",
        "claim": "The observed pages do not include the full discussion or limitations. Pages 8–25 are uninspected, and pages 9–10 are only partially available; conclusions about the paper’s full scope or limitations therefore remain open.",
        "page": 7,
        "quote": "In this section, we discuss several model behaviors and problem solving patterns"
      }
    ],
    "unresolved_questions": [
      "How do the authors characterize limitations and failure modes in the uninspected discussion and later pages?",
      "How robust are the Lite-set ablation differences to issue-subset variation, and do comparable interface gains hold beyond these two models and SWE-bench?",
      "How should the headline comparison with prior RAG results be interpreted given the differing model results and evaluation setups?"
    ]
  },
  "ledger": [
    {
      "check_id": "C1",
      "status": "mixed",
      "finding": "The paper reports GPT-4 Turbo resolving 12.47% (286/2,294) of the full SWE-bench test set. It also reports RAG results, including 3.79% for Claude 3 Opus, but that is not a same-model comparison with the GPT-4 Turbo headline. On Lite, the same-model GPT-4 shell-only result is 11.00% versus 18.00% for SWE-agent. Evaluation uses all-tests-pass as the resolution metric, with a $4 per-instance budget.",
      "support": ["E13", "E14"],
      "counterevidence": ["E14"],
      "unknown": "The supplied pages do not establish a directly comparable full-set, same-model RAG result for the headline comparison."
    },
    {
      "check_id": "C2",
      "status": "supported",
      "finding": "Lite-set GPT-4 ablations show lower resolved rates for shell-only editing, no linting, iterative search, alternative viewer sizes, and full history than for the reported SWE-agent configuration. The tested ablation set is 300 Lite instances; configuration selection also involved hand-picked development examples.",
      "support": ["E13", "E14", "E15"],
      "counterevidence": [],
      "unknown": "Robustness across other issue subsets and settings is not established by the inspected pages."
    },
    {
      "check_id": "C3",
      "status": "mixed",
      "finding": "The observed pages provide concrete methods, results, and ablations, but not the full discussion or limitations. The supplied coverage leaves pages 8 and 11–25 uninspected, with pages 9–10 partial; no claim about absent limitations is warranted.",
      "support": ["E1", "E3", "E13", "E14", "E15"],
      "counterevidence": ["E13", "E15"],
      "unknown": "The uninspected later-page discussion may add limitations, qualifications, or further evidence."
    }
  ],
  "changes": "Updated the evaluation assessment using the supplied pages 5–7: distinguished the cross-model RAG headline comparison from the same-model Lite shell-only comparison, and incorporated the reported ablation results and configuration-selection procedure. Replaced the first observation’s quote with an exact observed passage. The remaining discussion and limitations are still uninspected, so uncertainty is retained rather than treating that gap as a demonstrated defect."
}
